# HeyTony Image Tool: Scene Architecture Expansion

This plan outlines how we will expand the application from a single-purpose "EXIF Injector" into a multi-scene "Image Tool" that handles both Advanced Tagging and Rapid Multi-Format Conversions.

## User Review Required

> [!WARNING]  
> Modern Web Browsers natively support incredibly fast Canvas conversions for `PNG`, `JPEG`, and `WebP`. 
> 
> However, **`AVIF`** is still experimental in many browsers (like Safari and older versions of Chrome). The application will attempt to encode to `AVIF` if requested, but depending on the user's browser, it may silently fall back to standard `PNG` if the browser engine lacks an internal AVIF encoder. 
> 
> Is this acceptable for the "Quick Convert" tab, or would you like to exclude AVIF to guarantee perfect predictability?

## Proposed Architecture

We will introduce a React State for `activeScene` allowing you to flip between two distinct modes of operation without losing state.

### [MODIFY] `src/App.jsx`
- Introduce a top-level **Navigation Header** spanning the application.
- **Scene 1: Advanced Tagging** (The current layout).
- **Scene 2: Quick Convert**. A streamlined UI focused purely on massive drag-and-drop transcoding.
- Implement a simple "Target Format" dropdown/radio button for `Quick Convert` allowing you to select `.png`, `.jpg`, `.webp`, or `.avif`.

### [MODIFY] `src/ImageProcessor.js`
- Export a new, lightweight `quickConvert(file, targetFormat)` function.
- This will bypass all the complex piexif binary manipulation logic entirely, instantly pushing the Canvas rendering straight to your local Downloads folder using the requested MIME type (`image/jpeg`, `image/webp`, etc.).

## Verification Plan

### Automated Tests
- Validate that `quickConvert` successfully changes the file extension based on the selected target.

### Manual Verification
- We will rebuild the static HTML file and ensure the "Scene Toggle" flawlessly switches the layout back and forth without data loss.
- Perform a mass export of 3 images into `.webp` format natively.
